      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes and no. I looked into two ways of solving the \"optional\" part, and have settled on the more verbose approach for the moment.\n\nMy elegant solution was to add an \"optional\" or \"skip_in_read_only_mode\" argument to session_for_write() such that operations like updating a user\u0027s invalid password lockout count could be silently skipped when in read-only mode. The advantage to this approach is that any logic inside the context manager still gets executed, it just won\u0027t have an effect on the database.\n\n\n  def _increment_invalid_password_count(self):\n    with session_for_write(optional\u003dTrue) as session:\n      session.update_things()\n\nHowever, preventing writes from inside a context manager proved to be a little complicated (I was trying to stomp on the session\u0027s flush method with a no-op). I could get back to it, but we don\u0027t have many use cases for optional writes (yet), so it didn\u0027t seem to be worth the effort.\n\nInstead, we can write drivers with a conditional before grabbing a write session:\n\n  def _increment_invalid_password_count(self):\n    if not CONF.read_only_mode:\n      with session_for_write() as session:\n        session.update_things()",
      "parentUuid": "bacf61ea_52dc6174",
